  "notes": "After the defense takes the first 3 diamond tricks, and returns a heart, South must take the balance. South has a possible loser in both of the spade and club suits. South also has finessing positions in both of these suits.\n\nIf West has the ♠K, North's spade holding, will be worth 3 tricks by finessing twice. South will be able to discard 1 of his club losers on North's third spade. South will still have a club loser and will still need the finesse in that suit.\n\nIf South plays on clubs first he will make his contract if West has the ♣K, regardless of the position of the ♠K. If the club finesse wins, North's club holding is worth 4 tricks and South will have a discard for his spade loser. If East turns out to have the ♣K, the contract could not have been made.\n\nSouth should draw trump and play clubs through West's hoped for ♣K. South must be careful not to block the club suit. He should start with the ♣Q or ♣J on the first round of that suit.\n\nOn the second round, South should play the other high honour from his hand. The lead will still be in the South hand to take a third club finesse. The ♣A will remain to take care of South's spade loser. It would have done West no good to play the ♣K at any time.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "After the defense takes the first 3 diamond tricks, and returns a heart, South must take the balance. South has a possible loser in both of the spade and club suits. South also has finessing positions in both of these suits.",
    "If West has the ♠K, North's spade holding, will be worth 3 tricks by finessing twice. South will be able to discard 1 of his club losers on North's third spade. South will still have a club loser and will still need the finesse in that suit.",
    "If South plays on clubs first he will make his contract if West has the ♣K, regardless of the position of the ♠K. If the club finesse wins, North's club holding is worth 4 tricks and South will have a discard for his spade loser. If East turns out to have the ♣K, the contract could not have been made.",
    "South should draw trump and play clubs through West's hoped for ♣K. South must be careful not to block the club suit. He should start with the ♣Q or ♣J on the first round of that suit.",
    "On the second round, South should play the other high honour from his hand. The lead will still be in the South hand to take a third club finesse. The ♣A will remain to take care of South's spade loser. It would have done West no good to play the ♣K at any time."
  ],
